  urldate = {2026-05-07},
  abstract = {Recent progresses in asynchronous Byzantine fault-tolerant (BFT) consensus, e.g., \textbackslash mathsf Dumbo\textbackslash text -\textbackslash mathsf NGDumbo-NG (CCS’ 22) and \textbackslash mathsf TuskTusk (EuroSys’ 22), show promising performance through decoupling transaction dissemination and block agreement. However, when executed with a larger number nn of nodes, like several hundreds, they would suffer from significant degradation in performance. Their dominating scalability bottleneck is the huge authenticator complexity: each node has to multicast \textbackslash mathcal O(n)O(n) quorum certificates (QCs) and subsequently verify them for each block. This paper systematically investigates and resolves the above scalability issue. We first propose a signature-free asynchronous BFT consensus \textbackslash mathsf FIN\textbackslash text -\textbackslash mathsf NGFIN-NG that adapts a recent signature-free asynchronous common subset protocol FIN (CCS’ 23) into the state-of-the-art framework of concurrent broadcast and agreement. The liveness of \textbackslash mathsf FIN\textbackslash text -\textbackslash mathsf NGFIN-NG relies on our non-trivial redesign of FIN’s multi-valued validated Byzantine agreement towards achieving optimal quality. \textbackslash mathsf FIN\textbackslash text -\textbackslash mathsf NGFIN-NG greatly improves the performance of FIN and already outperforms \textbackslash mathsf Dumbo\textbackslash text -\textbackslash mathsf NGDumbo-NG in most deployment settings. To further overcome the scalability limit of \textbackslash mathsf FIN\textbackslash text -\textbackslash mathsf NGFIN-NG due to \textbackslash mathcal O(n\textasciicircum 3)O(n3) messages, we propose \textbackslash mathsf JUMBOJUMBO, a scalable instantiation of \textbackslash mathsf Dumbo\textbackslash text -\textbackslash mathsf NGDumbo-NG, with only \textbackslash mathcal O(n\textasciicircum 2)O(n2) complexities for both authenticators and messages. We use various aggregation and dispersal techniques for QCs to significantly reduce the authenticator complexity of original \textbackslash mathsf Dumbo\textbackslash text -\textbackslash mathsf NGDumbo-NG implementations by up to \textbackslash mathcal O(n\textasciicircum 2)O(n2) orders. Finally, we implement our designs in Golang and experimentally demonstrated their enhanced scalability with hundreds of Amazon’s AWS instances. \textbackslash mathsf JUMBOJUMBO and FIN-NG significantly outperform the state-of-the-art in (nearly) all deployment settings. Especially, when n\textbackslash gen≥196, \textbackslash mathsf JUMBOJUMBO can attain a throughput that is more than 4× that of FIN and \textbackslash mathsf Dumbo\textbackslash text -\textbackslash mathsf NGDumbo-NG.},
  langid = {english},
